WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:02.909
Film Courage: Trae you said you’ve been
here 15 years now from Chicago?

00:00:02.909 --> 00:00:03.909
Trae Ireland: Yes.

00:00:03.909 --> 00:00:07.460
Film Courage: If there had been a handbook
that someone was going to deliver to you and

00:00:07.460 --> 00:00:13.160
say “This is for you” and you will learn
all of this, but it’s going to take 15 years,

00:00:13.160 --> 00:00:14.600
what do you wish had been in this handbook?

00:00:14.600 --> 00:00:15.780
Trae Ireland: Money.

00:00:15.780 --> 00:00:16.780
[Laughs]

00:00:16.780 --> 00:00:17.780
Film Courage: Exactly.

00:00:17.780 --> 00:00:18.780
Okay.

00:00:18.780 --> 00:00:20.500
Trae Ireland: No…because you have to survive.

00:00:20.500 --> 00:00:21.980
Los Angeles is so expensive.

00:00:21.980 --> 00:00:26.660
If you are a struggling actor or anybody who
comes out here, the cost of living is so high

00:00:26.660 --> 00:00:30.949
you can’t…it’s hard for you to get a
regular job because you have to pursue this.

00:00:30.949 --> 00:00:34.030
You have to do auditions and meetings and
all this, so you’ll get fired or you’ll

00:00:34.030 --> 00:00:35.109
get laid off from your job.

00:00:35.109 --> 00:00:38.870
So you have to have money to be able to have
that cushion to be able to pursue this…but

00:00:38.870 --> 00:00:41.520
ask the question again so I can give you another
answer?

00:00:41.520 --> 00:00:42.520
Film Courage: Sure.

00:00:42.520 --> 00:00:43.520
But that’s good.

00:00:43.520 --> 00:00:44.520
I like that book actually.

00:00:44.520 --> 00:00:45.520
I wish someone had handed me that.

00:00:45.520 --> 00:00:48.870
But what else in that book could tell you
different things [such as] don’t waste your

00:00:48.870 --> 00:00:53.170
time with this, spend more time on that which
you wish someone had given to you that you’ve

00:00:53.170 --> 00:00:54.170
learned now where.

00:00:54.170 --> 00:00:55.170
And in turn, you could then hand this same
book to someone?

00:00:55.170 --> 00:00:59.179
Trae Ireland: Here is the irony of that because
you just never know.

00:00:59.179 --> 00:01:02.389
Because there is a lot of that when I first
got here I would try a lot of these showcases

00:01:02.389 --> 00:01:04.031
or whatever, don’t go to those.

00:01:04.031 --> 00:01:07.430
They are a rip-off because a lot of these
casting directors at these showcases they

00:01:07.430 --> 00:01:08.920
are trying to make their own money and pay
their own bills.

00:01:08.920 --> 00:01:13.010
And they are not really invested to find somebody
but if they do “Oh!

00:01:13.010 --> 00:01:14.860
I just found this guy.”

00:01:14.860 --> 00:01:15.860
So it’s so unorthodox.

00:01:15.860 --> 00:01:24.310
There is not a set blueprint so to have that
book…it’s just experiences.

00:01:24.310 --> 00:01:28.369
It should be like a book of just experiences
and you just learn.

00:01:28.369 --> 00:01:33.859
That’s why I watch all those backstories
on everybody.

00:01:33.859 --> 00:01:38.039
The most important thing is just to commit
to whatever it’s going to be that you are

00:01:38.039 --> 00:01:39.039
going to do.

00:01:39.039 --> 00:01:43.200
And that is to do your research to whatever
the character’s going to be, whatever you

00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:44.200
choose.

00:01:44.200 --> 00:01:45.200
I don’t care if you want to work at The
Home Depot.

00:01:45.200 --> 00:01:48.920
If you want to be the head cashier you have
to commit to whatever it is.

00:01:48.920 --> 00:01:50.720
Put the hours in, the work.

00:01:50.720 --> 00:01:57.470
So that book is mainly just to stay focused
on what you want to do and just enjoy the

00:01:57.470 --> 00:02:01.729
experience, even if the bad things that are
going to happen to you shapes and molds you

00:02:01.729 --> 00:02:02.729
to who you are.

00:02:02.729 --> 00:02:05.189
Think about the things in life where you are
like “I would never do that again.”

00:02:05.189 --> 00:02:10.420
But it still prepares you to be on the lookout
for anything else or to do anything else.

00:02:10.420 --> 00:02:16.580
Even if I was to write a book right now, I
think it would be very informative but at

00:02:16.580 --> 00:02:19.010
the same time it’s everybody else’s journey.

00:02:19.010 --> 00:02:25.300
There is nothing in particular I can give
from that except get as much knowledge as

00:02:25.300 --> 00:02:25.800
you can.

